// §5 "Worth knowing" — the interruption block. Callout, tone="note".
//
// The lever for this reader is the BFD's own consequence framing: what
// inaction costs an agency is not a dirty property, it is rework and
// reputational exposure. The cheapest quote is the one most likely to
// produce a second inspection, and a second inspection costs a property
// manager a morning they do not have.
const worthKnowing = {
  heading: 'The cheap vacancy clean is not cheap. It is a second inspection.',
  body: [
    'A clean that comes in eighty dollars under still has to pass someone else’s written report. When it does not, the cost is not the eighty dollars — it is your re-inspection, the days the property sits unlisted, the landlord asking why, and the tenant who now has a dispute with you rather than with the cleaner.',
    'That is why agencies here tend to procure on capacity and communication rather than on price, and it is why we would rather be trialled on a hard property than a cheap one.',
  ],
};

// §6 Why sole operators fail on a rent roll. TextBlock dark — the
// long-form persuasion slot the sibling pages all carry.
//
// Written as what a property manager should ask, not as what TLB claims.
// That register is the commercial hub's, and it is the one that survives a
// reader who has been sold to before.
const whyTeamsBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Most cleaners who take agency work are one person with a car, and for a while that is fine — they are often excellent, and they are cheap. The problem is structural rather than personal. A rent roll produces vacancies in clusters, at the end of the month and around the school terms, and one person can physically do one property a day. So the week you have four exits is the week you find out that your reliable cleaner has three of them and someone else has to take the fourth, on Thursday, with the photos booked for Friday.',
  'Then there is what happens when they are sick, or on holiday, or they take a full-time job — which is common, because cleaning is one of the highest-turnover trades in the country. When a sole operator stops, your entire cleaning arrangement stops with them, and you are back to ringing around with a vacancy already on the board.',
  'Three questions sort it out quickly, and they are worth asking us as readily as anyone else. How many people do you actually employ, as opposed to subcontract? What happens to my job when the person who normally does it is away? And who answers the phone at four on a Friday when the keys have come back and the property is worse than the notes said?',
];

