      // Approved claim: the comparison table's first row, and the BFD's
      // headline differentiator for the business as a whole.
      'Employed. Nine full-time cleaners on our own team, not contractors booked through a platform, which is why we can guarantee who turns up and cover a property when someone is away. We are [insured] and [police-checked], and we are a local business rather than a franchise routing your call somewhere else.',
  },
];

// §12 Non-branded FAQs — the long-tail block, aimed at what agencies and
// landlords search rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and FAQ 2 is the sensitive one. What a
// tenancy agreement can require of an outgoing tenant is set by residential
// tenancy law and differs between NSW and QLD, and it is contested — carpet
// cleaning and professional-cleaning clauses in particular. It is answered
// here at the level of "there are limits, and here is where to check" and
// points at the guide the nav already promises, rather than stating a rule.
// Do not "improve" it by adding a specific without a compliance read.
//
// The guide link (/guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/)
// is taken verbatim from navigation.ts, where it is listed and unbuilt. It
// will 404 until that guide ships.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a vacancy clean and a routine clean?',
    answer:
      'A routine clean maintains a property somebody lives in: surfaces, floors, bathrooms and kitchen, on a schedule. A vacancy or exit clean resets an empty property to the standard a written condition report is measured against, which brings in everything a routine clean reasonably leaves alone — inside the oven and rangehood filter, window tracks and sills, fan blades, light fittings, skirtings, inside cupboards and wardrobes, walls where they are marked. It is a different scope and a different price, and it takes two to four times as long.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a tenant be required to have the property professionally cleaned?',
    answer:
      'Not as broadly as many agreements imply. In both NSW and Queensland a tenant’s obligation is generally to leave the property in the same condition it was in at the start, fair wear and tear excepted — which is a comparison against the entry condition report rather than a professional-cleaning receipt. Blanket clauses requiring professional cleaning or professional carpet cleaning regardless of condition are the ones most often challenged, and pets can change the position. This is general information rather than advice: check the current requirements with NSW Fair Trading or the Queensland RTA, and read our guide on what the law actually says.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who pays for the bond clean — the tenant, the landlord or the agency?',
    answer:
      'Usually the outgoing tenant arranges and pays for it, because it is their obligation to return the property in the agreed condition. The agency arranges and bills it to the owner where the tenant has not done it, where the tenant has vacated without cleaning, or where it is a make-ready between tenancies rather than an exit obligation. Deducting a cleaning cost from a bond is a separate process with its own evidence requirements, and it needs the condition report to support it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a vacancy clean take?',
    answer:
      'A two-bedroom unit is usually four to six hours for one cleaner. A four-bedroom house with two bathrooms is a full day, or half a day with two or three cleaners on it, which is how a same-week turnaround gets done. Carpet cleaning and exterior washing are separate jobs on top, and carpet needs drying time before the photographer arrives — which is the scheduling trap on a tight vacancy.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should an agency ask a cleaner before adding them to a preferred trades list?',
    answer:
      'Five things, and none of them is the price. How many cleaners do you employ rather than subcontract. What happens to my job when the usual person is away. What is your commitment if an exit inspection fails on cleaning. Are you insured, and can I see the certificate of currency. And how do you invoice — per property, referenced how, on what terms. A cleaner who answers all five without hedging is a different proposition from the cheapest quote.',
  },
];

// §13 Closing CTA. This is why-tlb.md §10's approved copy, picked up
// verbatim, because that page sends this reader here on exactly that
// promise. A page that is linked with a specific offer and then does not
// make it reads as a bait.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Real estate and property management cleaning',
  heading: 'Give us the difficult property.',
  lead: 'The one with the tight window or the awkward exit condition. That is the job that tells you something — and it is a better trial than the easy one. No lock-in, no obligation.',
  cta: { label: 'Book a call with TLB', href: quoteCta.href },
};
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid
        across both sections. 75% keeps Dark Teal body copy above 4.5:1 over
        the darkest part of the frame. Same treatment as every sibling. */}
    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={pathwayCards.heading} headingAccent={pathwayCards.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards.cards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading="What working with us looks like"
      lead="An agency is not buying a clean. It is buying not having to think about the clean, so this is the operational side rather than the task list."
      items={workingWithUsItems}
      cta={{ label: 'Book a call with TLB', href: quoteCta.href }}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={worthKnowing.heading} body={worthKnowing.body} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading="Why a sole operator eventually fails on a rent roll"
      body={whyTeamsBody}
    />

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair. These
        four images are SAMPLES — the `note` is what tells the reader that;
        delete it with the imports. See the §7 comment for the photo brief
        and the consent conditions. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading="The standard an inspection is measured against."
      lead="Drag the handle across each photo to see the same spot before and after a clean."
      pairs={beforeAfterPairs}
      note="Illustrative samples, shown while we collect before and after photos from real TLB cleans."
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: agency logo wall (LogoBar.astro, variant="list").

        This is the strongest proof available to this page and to the site.
        The trust bar above already claims "Preferred supplier for real
        estate agencies in the region" in words; the marks would make it
        checkable, and BFD §"Client and partner proof" specifically notes
        that competitors display agency logos and TLB should seek permission
        to reference its agency relationships.

        NOT BUILT because permission is required per mark, at principal
        level, and none has been recorded. A logo wall assembled without it
        is both a trademark problem and the exact "fake proof"
        IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 rules out. Drop it in here, between the
        before/after and the service pills, once the permissions exist. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading="What we do for agencies"
      subheading="Across the Northern Rivers, the Tweed and the Southern Gold Coast."
      tags={serviceTags}
      note={["Something not on the list? Ask us anyway."]}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={comparisonTable.heading}
      cornerLabel={comparisonTable.cornerLabel}
      lead={comparisonTable.lead}
      columns={comparisonTable.columns}
      rows={comparisonTable.rows}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What property managers say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading="Northern Rivers, the Tweed and the Southern Gold Coast."
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    <Faq heading="Frequently Asked Questions" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Real Estate Cleaning FAQs" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
